{"name":"Glowing Firelight","hex":"#af5941","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glowing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of glow"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The action of glowing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glowing"},{"term":"firelight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The light of a fire, such as from a campfire or fireplace."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/firelight"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phosphorescence","blocks":[{"text":"Phosphorescence is a type of photoluminescence related to fluorescence. When exposed to light (radiation) of a shorter threshold wavelength, a phosphorescent substance will glow, absorbing the light and reemitting it at a longer wavelength. Unlike fluorescence, a phosphorescent material does not immediately reemit the radiation it absorbs. Instead, a phosphorescent material absorbs some of the radiation energy and reemits it for a much longer time after the radiation source is removed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Phosphorescence.jpg/330px-Phosphorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorescence"},{"title":"Firelight","blocks":[{"text":"Firelight is a 1997 period romance film written and directed by William Nicholson and starring Sophie Marceau and Stephen Dillane. The film is about a woman who agrees to bear the child of an anonymous English landowner in return for payment to resolve her father's debts. When the child is born, the woman gives up the child as agreed. Seven years later, the woman is hired as a governess to a girl on a remote Sussex estate. The girl's father is the anonymous landowner. Filmed on location in Firle, England and Calvados, France, the film premiered at the Deauville American Film Festival on 14 September 1997. Firelight was Nicholson's first film as a director."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c5/Firelight_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firelight"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/500px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/1280px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/500px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Phosphorescent, europium-doped, strontium silicate-aluminate oxide powder under visible light, fluorescing/phosphorescing under long-wave UV light, and persistently phosphorescing in total darkness","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhosphorescent.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/JablonskiSimple.png/500px-JablonskiSimple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/JablonskiSimple.png/500px-JablonskiSimple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jablonski diagram of an energy scheme used to explain the difference between fluorescence and phosphorescence. The excitation of molecule A to its singlet excited state (1A*) may, after a short time between absorption and emission (fluorescence lifetime), return immediately to ground state, giving off a photon via fluorescence (decay time). However, sustained excitation is followed by intersystem crossing to the triplet state (3A) that relaxes to the ground state by phosphorescence with much longer decay times.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJablonskiSimple.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/500px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/1280px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/500px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"After an electron absorbs a photon of high energy, it may undergo vibrational relaxations and intersystem crossing to another spin state.  Again the system relaxes vibrationally in the new spin state and eventually emits light by phosphorescence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElectronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/500px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/1280px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/500px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An extremely intense pulse of short-wave UV light in a flashtube produced this blue persistent-phosphorescence in the amorphous, fused silica envelope, lasting as long as 20 minutes after the 3.5 microsecond flash.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/MoS2_vacancies.jpg/500px-MoS2_vacancies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/MoS2_vacancies.jpg/500px-MoS2_vacancies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An electron microscope reveals vacancy defects in a crystalline lattice of molybdenum disulfide. The missing sulfur atoms leave dangling bonds between the molybdenum atoms, creating traps in the empty spaces.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoS2_vacancies.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg/120px-Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg/250px-Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg/120px-Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg"},{"sr